454 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (After delivering the Toyota Hilux truck I stayed in Kramatorsk for another two nights to see how the situation has changed since my previous trip. And changed it has: as you will see, Russia is radically ramping up its efforts to destroy the Kramatorsk-Sloviansk area and force the civilian population out. It's a significantly more dangerous place to be than even a month ago.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
455 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (But first things first: I still hadn't found them the Starlink they wanted. So we tracked down a store that had a single Starlink mini in stock at one of the main shopping areas:)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
456 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (21,900 UAH. A bit of a premium over the typical cost in Ukraine... But I mean, look at that building. About half of those missing windows are new, since the last time I had visited just a month prior, due yet another glide bomb \(KAB\) attack by Russia that landed on the road just ~15m away.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
457 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (The unit needed the Starlink right away, so we got some fuel for my friend's perfectly normal, civilian car, at a perfectly normal, civilian, gas station:)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
458 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (Yes, it's open. Note the guy refilling his LPG car from the LPG tank hidden in the bunker; the one out in the open is definitely empty. Gas stations have been a priority target for Russia, as fuel distribution is critical to the civilian population; that gas station had been hit before.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
459 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (I didn't get footage of us actually delivering the Starlink, as we took it to a sensitive location kinda close to the front lines. Close enough that my friend was teaching me the basics of defensive driving within fiber drone range: how to check corners for ambush drones while turning corners. Pretty simple concept: driver checks his side for drones; passenger simultaneously checks the other side. If you actually see a drone, one option is to jump out of the vehicle and hope that the goes for the high value vehicle rather than you. That's why they didn't care that the driver side seatbelt in the Toyota Hilux was broken...)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
460 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (With the Starlink delivered, I got to tag along with a medical officer in the unit while he checked in with some foreign volunteers from the USA who were taking time off in Kramatorsk to recover; we literally met them in a coffee shop. Concussions from near-miss encounters with drones are a serious issue. And as you can see by their patches, they took the issue very seriously:)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
461 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (But seriously, all joking aside, the Ukrainian military medical system is using the latest, most advanced, concussion protocols available to treat soldiers, informed by the US's experiences in Iraq and Afghanistan.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
